 HTML props:"},"9":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-7","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started","content":"For the smallest CSS footprint, import individual components:"},"10":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-8","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#nextjs-and-ssr","content":"Import shared styles in `app/layout.tsx`. Published component entries declare their client boundary, so server components can import them. Put state and event handlers in your own `'use client'` component. Viewport hooks initially return `null`; handle that state without reading `window` during rendering. Modal content uses portals and appears after mounting."},"11":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-9","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-with-twill","content":"You can also write SwiftUI.js views in Twill, our sibling Swift-inspired language for JavaScript and TypeScript. Follow Twill's React integration guide to configure `.twillx` compilation. Component imports, React state, props and the shared-style import above stay the same."},"12":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-10","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#make-it-yours","content":"Pass `className`, `style`, HTML attributes and `aria-*` props. Override CSS variables at an application root or theme boundary; Tailwind can style your application without becoming a dependency of the component library."},"13":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-11","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#make-it-yours","content":"Try the workspace demo, read responsive behavior, and check the capability matrix before adopting experimental components."},"14":{"id":"/docs","page_id":"/docs","type":"page","content":"Overview","tags":[],"url":"/docs"},"15":{"id":"/docs-0","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"SwiftUI-inspired composition, built for React and the web."},"16":{"id":"/docs-1","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#related-project-twill","content":"Related project: Twill"},"17":{"id":"/docs-2","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#read-the-limits-first","content":"Read the limits first"},"18":{"id":"/docs-3","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Build screens with ordinary React state, HTML props and composable layout components."},"19":{"id":"/docs-4","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Start here"},"20":{"id":"/docs-5","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"What you will find"},"21":{"id":"/docs-6","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Getting started"},"22":{"id":"/docs-7","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Installation, required styles and a working first component"},"23":{"id":"/docs-8","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Components"},"24":{"id":"/docs-9","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Live examples, copyable code, requirements and complete APIs"},"25":{"id":"/docs-10","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Responsive layouts"},"26":{"id":"/docs-11","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Container breakpoints, layout hints and SSR fallbacks"},"27":{"id":"/docs-12","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Themes and materials"},"28":{"id":"/docs-13","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Scoped appearance, tokens and optional glass"},"29":{"id":"/docs-14","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Capability matrix"},"30":{"id":"/docs-15","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Supported behavior, experimental features and platform limits"},"31":{"id":"/docs-16","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Kitchensink"},"32":{"id":"/docs-17","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"An integrated form, navigation and modal workflow"},"33":{"id":"/docs-18","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#related-project-twill","content":"Twill is our sibling Swift-inspired language for JavaScript and TypeScript. Follow its React integration guide to write SwiftUI.js views in `.twillx`, keeping ordinary component props, React state and shared styles. SwiftUI.js also works with standard TypeScript and JSX. See the Twill repository for the compiler and tooling."},"34":{"id":"/docs-19","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#read-the-limits-first","content":"SwiftUI.js uses browser rendering. It does not reproduce every native SwiftUI API or Apple system behavior. Read a component's usage notes before adopting it; experimental exports may change."},"35":{"id":"/docs-20","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#read-the-limits-first","content":"Status terms: **aligned** follows SwiftUI intent closely; **adapted** uses browser-specific behavior; **partial** has documented gaps; **planned** is not shipped behavior. These describe scope, not a guarantee of production acceptance. Validate the components you use in your target browsers."},"36":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","type":"page","content":"Capability Matrix","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix"},"37":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","content":"Core components, web adaptations, and experimental capabilities."},"38":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Start with the core"},"39":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#web-adaptations","content":"Web adaptations"},"40":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#experimental-capabilities","content":"Experimental capabilities"},"41":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#release-acceptance","content":"Release acceptance"},"42":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","content":"SwiftUI.js carries SwiftUI's composition ideas into React. It is an evolving web library: native Apple rendering and complete SwiftUI API parity are outside its current contract."},"43":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Build everyday screens with `Button`, `Text`, `TextField`, `SecureField`, `TextEditor`, `Toggle`, `Picker`, `Slider`, `Stepper`, `ProgressView`, `HStack`, `VStack`, `ZStack`, `Spacer`, `Divider`, `Grid`, `GridRow`, `List`, `Section`, `Form`, `DisclosureGroup`, `LabeledContent`, `Sheet`, `Alert`, and `ConfirmationDialog`."},"44":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"These are the priority acceptance surface. “Core” identifies development focus; it does not claim that every component has completed cross-browser, accessibility and visual acceptance."},"45":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Area"},"46":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Current behavior"},"47":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Limit"},"48":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Forms"},"49":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-12","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Native HTML controls; ordinary React events and controlled props"},"50":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-13","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Applications provide validation, labels and persistence"},"51":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-14","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Modals"},"52":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-15","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Portal, focus trap and restoration, background isolation, scroll lock, nested Escape handling"},"53":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-16","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Handle supports pointer dragging, detent snapping and dismissal; content scrolls separately"},"54":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-17","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Motion"},"55":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-18","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Paired modal/menu transitions, sheet detent settling, disclosure expansion, content-update feedback and system Reduce Motion"},"56":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-19","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Web timing curves approximate settling; native physics springs and text/number interpolation are not implemented. Motion guide"},"57":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-20","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Adaptive layout"},"58":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-21","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"CSS stacks and grids; container-aware `NavigationSplitView` with compact column navigation"},"59":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-22","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Two columns collapse below 768px; three below 1024px; `compact` overrides this"},"60":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-23","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"SSR"},"61":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-24","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Client-marked package entries can be prerendered; navigation IDs are stable"},"62":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-25","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Viewport hooks return `null` on the server; portal content appears after mounting"},"63":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-26","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Packaging"},"64":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-27","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Component CSS, strict consumer types and unused JavaScript removal are checked from the packed artifact"},"65":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-28","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#start-with-the-core","content":"Import shared styles once; use component subpaths for the smallest CSS footprint"},"66":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-29","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#web-adaptations","content":"`NavigationStack`, `TabView`, menus, popovers, dates, charts and platform bridges retain web-specific behavior. Review each reference before relying on keyboard interactions, gestures, media permissions or fallback behavior."},"67":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-30","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#web-adaptations","content":"`ViewThatFits` observes container width and selects children using `data-min-width` hints. It does not measure intrinsic SwiftUI layout proposals. Its first child renders during SSR; explicit `width` makes selection deterministic."},"68":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-31","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#web-adaptations","content":"`LazyVStack`, `LazyHStack`, `LazyVGrid`, and `LazyHGrid` share measured viewport virtualization for overflow containers and page scrolling. Estimates are corrected as items resize, and focused rows remain mounted. Off-screen rows unmount, so keep editable state outside each item. SSR emits an initial window rather than the whole dataset."},"69":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-32","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#experimental-capabilities","content":"Presentation, scroll and animation modifier wrappers may expose metadata without implementing the corresponding native system behavior."},"70":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-33","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#experimental-capabilities","content":"Maps, media and canvas bridges need application-specific integration checks."},"71":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-34","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#experimental-capabilities","content":"Existing exports remain available. Experimental APIs may change as behavior is implemented. Component count is not an acceptance metric."},"72":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-35","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#release-acceptance","content":"The repository runs lint, type checks, tests and builds. The packed-consumer check additionally validates strict TypeScript declarations, client boundaries, SSR across separate processes, CSS retention and a 2 KiB gzipped JavaScript budget for an isolated `Button` (React excluded)."},"73":{"id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix-36","page_id":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/capability-matrix#release-acceptance","content":"Production acceptance still requires browser interaction, responsive, accessibility and visual review for the components your application uses. The kitchensink provides a compact integrated form and modal workflow; Storybook covers the wider surface."},"74":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","type":"page","content":"Motion","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/motion"},"75":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion","content":"Brief transitions, responsive gestures and reduced-motion behavior."},"76":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"What moves"},"77":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/motion#customize-timing","content":"Customize timing"},"78":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/motion#reduce-motion","content":"Reduce Motion"},"79":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion","content":"Motion helps people follow state changes. SwiftUI.js follows Apple's motion guidance: keep feedback brief, follow the gesture and make motion optional."},"80":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Surface"},"81":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Behavior"},"82":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Sheet and confirmation dialog"},"83":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Enter from the bottom and leave in the same direction; desktop form sheets use a subtle scale and fade"},"84":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Alert, menu and popover"},"85":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Subtle scale and fade; closing stays mounted until the transition finishes"},"86":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Sheet handle"},"87":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-12","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Follows the pointer directly; settles to the selected detent after release"},"88":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-13","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"DisclosureGroup"},"89":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-14","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Animates content height and the disclosure chevron in both directions"},"90":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-15","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Toggle and controls"},"91":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-16","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Brief state feedback; native input interactions remain browser-controlled"},"92":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-17","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"ContentTransition"},"93":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-18","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Fades changed content; `scale` also settles from 96% to its authored size"},"94":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-19","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#what-moves","content":"Keep controlled presentations mounted while changing `isPresented`/`isVisible`; conditionally removing the component bypasses its exit. Popover and menu can reopen while closing. Navigation handles canceled, zero-duration and missing animations without waiting indefinitely."},"95":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-20","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#customize-timing","content":"Defaults use 150–350ms transitions, with a 320ms presentation and a 200ms dismissal. These are web design choices, not Apple's published timing constants. Duration tokens require `ms` or `s` units. Set shared CSS tokens through `UIProvider`:"},"96":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-21","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#customize-timing","content":"Navigation links accept `pageOptions.transition` with `slide`, `fade`, `scale`, `none` or `view-transition`, plus `duration` in milliseconds and a CSS `easing`. View Transitions use browser snapshots and shared CSS motion tokens; unsupported browsers fall back to a CSS slide. Per-page duration/easing apply to CSS page animations."},"97":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-22","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#reduce-motion","content":"The system's `prefers-reduced-motion` preference removes component transitions and repeating CSS effects. Loading indicators keep a static accessible status. `PhaseAnimator` and `KeyframeAnimator` pause automatic progression; manual controls remain usable. Preference changes are observed while mounted. The `useReducedMotion()` hook is also exported for application-authored motion."},"98":{"id":"/docs/concepts/motion-23","page_id":"/docs/concepts/motion","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/motion#reduce-motion","content":"CSS curves approximate settling. Native SwiftUI spring physics, interpolated text/numbers, scroll-driven modifier behavior and automatic matched-geometry coordination remain outside the current implementation. `ContentTransition` uses opacity for `interpolate` and shows changes immediately when Web Animations are unavailable."},"99":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","type":"page","content":"Performance","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/performance"},"100":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance","content":"Smaller imports, focused subscriptions and predictable scrolling."},"101":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/performance#import-what-you-use","content":"Import what you use"},"102":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/performance#subscribe-to-the-state-you-need","content":"Subscribe to the state you need"},"103":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/performance#keep-long-collections-bounded","content":"Keep long collections bounded"},"104":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/performance#validate-your-application","content":"Validate your application"},"105":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#import-what-you-use","content":"The root entry is convenient and includes component styles across the library. JavaScript tree shaking removes unused components, but CSS remains. For a smaller stylesheet, use component subpaths and import shared styles once:"},"106":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#import-what-you-use","content":"Hooks can also use their module subpath, such as `@swiftuijs/ui/contexts/size-class`, without loading the root entry's styles. Importing anything else from the root entry still brings those styles into the bundle. No Tailwind setup is required."},"107":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#subscribe-to-the-state-you-need","content":"Use `useHorizontalSizeClass` or `useVerticalSizeClass` for breakpoint decisions. They return `null` during SSR and initial hydration and update only when the selected axis changes classification. Use `useViewport` or `useSizeClass` when you need current pixel dimensions. Prefer CSS for purely visual adaptation."},"108":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#subscribe-to-the-state-you-need","content":"Layout contexts keep a stable value until their direction changes. React can skip expensive children when their props are stable; memoization still depends on your application's props and children."},"109":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#keep-long-collections-bounded","content":"Lazy stacks and grids virtualize mounted rows. Keep editable state outside rows, use stable keys and choose an estimate close to the real item size. `List` and `Table` render all their children or rows. Lazy layouts still receive the complete `children` collection: creating those React elements can remain expensive for very large data sets."},"110":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#keep-long-collections-bounded","content":"Floating controls coalesce scroll, resize and observer bursts into animation frames. Their opening position is measured immediately; closing removes pending work. Large glass surfaces, blur and height-changing animations can still cost layout or painting time. Glass is opt-in. Refraction maps are cached (24 geometries), capped at 131,072 pixels and regenerated after a 120 ms resize pause; CSS is used while resizing. Repeated surfaces still add browser compositing cost, so measure realistic content and device sizes before increasing its intensity or repeating it throughout a page."},"111":{"id":"/docs/concepts/performance-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/performance#validate-your-application","content":"Profile a production build while typing, resizing, opening overlays and scrolling large collections. Check React commits and browser layout/paint time on your target devices. The repository's `test:performance` command checks render counts, shared subscriptions and geometry-read budgets; `test:package` checks JavaScript tree shaking and CSS gzip budgets. These checks prevent specific regressions and do not guarantee a frame rate on every browser or device."},"112":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","type":"page","content":"Responsive Design","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design"},"113":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","content":"Container-aware layouts and predictable server rendering."},"114":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#adaptive-split-navigation","content":"Adaptive split navigation"},"115":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#viewport-hooks","content":"Viewport hooks"},"116":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#check-your-composition","content":"Check your composition"},"117":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","content":"Stacks and grids use CSS layout. Give containers room to shrink, wrap long text, and avoid fixed widths larger than the available space."},"118":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#adaptive-split-navigation","content":"`NavigationSplitView` observes its own container. Two columns collapse below 768px; three collapse below 1024px. Compact navigation lets users reach every column. Set `defaultCompactColumn` to choose the initial screen, or control `compactColumn` and `onCompactColumnChange` with React state. Set `compact` explicitly to override automatic adaptation."},"119":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#adaptive-split-navigation","content":"`ViewThatFits` also observes its container, but requires `data-min-width` hints. Order variants from spacious to compact. Explicit `width` overrides observation; intrinsic content measurement is not implemented."},"120":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#viewport-hooks","content":"`useViewport`, `useSizeClass`, `useHorizontalSizeClass` and `useVerticalSizeClass` share one resize subscription, removed when the last subscriber unmounts. A viewport narrower than 768px is horizontally compact; below 667px high it is vertically compact. These are web breakpoints, not Apple's device classification rules."},"121":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#viewport-hooks","content":"All four hooks return `null` on the server and during initial hydration. Prefer CSS for visual adaptation; when rendering depends on a hook, provide a stable fallback for `null`."},"122":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#viewport-hooks","content":"Use an axis hook when you only need a breakpoint. It updates when that axis crosses the breakpoint; `useSizeClass` and `useViewport` also update their pixel dimensions during resizing."},"123":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#check-your-composition","content":"`Sheet` fills the width and attaches to the bottom below 768px, including wider touch screens under 500px high (phone landscape). Other wide viewports center page and form sheets with width caps of 600px and 420px. `fullScreen` always fills the viewport. Alert, confirmation dialog and menu content scrolls when the viewport is short."},"124":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#check-your-composition","content":"For edge-to-edge mobile pages, set `viewport-fit=cover` in the viewport meta tag so browsers expose `env(safe-area-inset-*)`. Sheet content and confirmation dialogs account for those insets. Embedded hosts can supply `--safe-area-top`, `--safe-area-right`, `--safe-area-bottom`, and `--safe-area-left` instead. These are Web presentation rules; native SwiftUI adapts by platform, OS version and size class."},"125":{"id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design-12","page_id":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/responsive-design#check-your-composition","content":"Test narrow phones, tablets and desktop containers, including long labels, enlarged text, keyboard navigation and dark mode. Container width can be much smaller than viewport width when a component appears in a sidebar or dialog."},"126":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","type":"page","content":"Layout and alignment","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment"},"127":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Choose a direction, cross-axis alignment and spacing."},"128":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment#usage-and-limitations","content":"Usage and limitations"},"129":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"SwiftUI.js stacks use CSS flexbox; grids use CSS grid. They follow SwiftUI's composition model with browser layout rules."},"130":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Container"},"131":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Direction"},"132":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Cross-axis alignment"},"133":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"`HStack`"},"134":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Horizontal row"},"135":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"`top`, `center`, `bottom`"},"136":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"`VStack`"},"137":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Vertical column"},"138":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"`leading`, `center`, `trailing`"},"139":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-12","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"`ZStack`"},"140":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-13","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Overlapping layers"},"141":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-14","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","content":"Combined horizontal/vertical alignment"},"142":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-15","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment#usage-and-limitations","content":"`spacing` controls gaps between adjacent children; use shared tokens for consistent spacing."},"143":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-16","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment#usage-and-limitations","content":"`Spacer` expands only when the parent has available space along the stack's main axis."},"144":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-17","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment#usage-and-limitations","content":"A row does not wrap automatically. Allow wrapping explicitly or switch composition for compact containers."},"145":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-18","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment#usage-and-limitations","content":"CSS shrink/stretch rules apply. Avoid deeply nested wrappers added only to force alignment; check long labels and enlarged text."},"146":{"id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment-19","page_id":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/swiftui-alignment#usage-and-limitations","content":"Native layout proposals and measurement behavior are not emulated. See responsive layouts for container adaptation."},"147":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","type":"page","content":"Themes and materials","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/theming"},"148":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming","content":"Scoped appearance configuration and an optional Liquid Glass-inspired Web material."},"149":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/theming#theme-and-tokens","content":"Theme and tokens"},"150":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/theming#glass-configuration","content":"Glass configuration"},"151":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts/theming#requirements-and-fallbacks","content":"Requirements and fallbacks"},"152":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming","content":"Wrap a subtree in `UIProvider`. Theme changes stay scoped, including portaled dialogs; nested providers inherit unspecified options."},"153":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#theme-and-tokens","content":"`theme` accepts `light`, `dark`, or `system`. CSS resolves the system preference without changing the server markup. `tokens` accepts `--sw-*` CSS variables; you can also override them in your own stylesheet. Keep accent colors readable in both themes."},"154":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#glass-configuration","content":"Liquid Glass is off by default. `glass` accepts a boolean or `{ enabled, intensity, variant, renderer }`; intensity is clamped to 0–1 and zero disables the effect. The preference applies to `Glass`, menus, context menus, popovers, navigation controls, toolbar groups, tab bars, alerts, confirmation dialogs and automatic sheets. Each supports a local `glass` override. Keep forms and content cards opaque."},"155":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#glass-configuration","content":"Presentations default to regular material for legibility, even with a global clear preference. An automatic sheet becomes more opaque at its large detent; full-screen sheets stay opaque. Explicit `Sheet.backgroundStyle` values take precedence over glass preferences and continue to support opaque accessibility fallbacks."},"156":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#requirements-and-fallbacks","content":"The regular variant softens background detail and favors legibility. Clear uses less blur and a much lighter tint so the background stays visible. Use clear only over visually rich backgrounds and verify contrast; bright media may need a dimming layer behind the control. Chromium adds SVG edge refraction that follows the surface's size and corner radii, with clear foreground content. Safari and Firefox retain CSS blur, tint and rim highlights. Use `glass={{ renderer: 'css' }}` to choose CSS everywhere; `auto` is the default. Apple's system vibrancy and full native optical model are not reproduced."},"157":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#requirements-and-fallbacks","content":"SSR renders the CSS fallback; the optional renderer loads after hydration. SVG maps require `img-src data:` under CSP; blocked images or a failed optional chunk keep the CSS fallback. Maps use a bounded cache and rebuild after resizing settles, with no graphics engine or continuously running animation."},"158":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#requirements-and-fallbacks","content":"Reduced transparency, increased contrast and forced colors fall back to an opaque surface. Browsers without backdrop-filter also retain an opaque background. Test the material with your actual content, and use `glass={false}` for surfaces where effects are unnecessary."},"159":{"id":"/docs/concepts/theming-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts/theming","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts/theming#requirements-and-fallbacks","content":"See Apple's material guidelines for the distinction between the functional glass layer and standard content materials."},"160":{"id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","page_id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","type":"page","content":"ActivityIndicator","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Components"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/activityindicator"},"161":{"id":"/docs/components/activityindicator-0","page_id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/activityindicator","content":"Show that an asynchronous task is in progress."},"162":{"id":"/docs/components/activityindicator-1","page_id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/activityindicator#usage-and-limitations","content":"Usage and limitations"},"163":{"id":"/docs/components/activityindicator-2","page_id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/activityindicator#usage-and-limitations","content":"Sizes are `small`, `medium`, or `large`; `color` overrides the spinner color."},"164":{"id":"/docs/components/activityindicator-3","page_id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/activityindicator#usage-and-limitations","content":"The spinner is visual and non-interactive. Provide nearby task text and an accessible status label."},"165":{"id":"/docs/components/activityindicator-4","page_id":"/docs/components/activityindicator","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/activityindicator#usage-and-limitations","content":"Place it where the awaited content will appear; use ProgressView when you know the amount completed."},"166":{"id":"/docs/components/alert","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","type":"page","content":"Alert","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Components"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/alert"},"167":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-0","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert","content":"Present a short message that requires attention or a decision."},"168":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-1","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/alert#simple-alert","content":"Simple Alert"},"169":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-2","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/alert#usage-and-limitations","content":"Usage and limitations"},"170":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-3","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert#simple-alert","content":"Alert without a message:"},"171":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-4","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert#usage-and-limitations","content":"Control visibility with `isVisible` and close from `onDismiss`."},"172":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-5","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert#usage-and-limitations","content":"Inherits the `UIProvider` glass toggle and intensity with regular material for legibility. Use `glass={false}` for an opaque local override. Accessibility preferences and unsupported browsers restore an opaque surface."},"173":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-6","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert#usage-and-limitations","content":"Buttons support `default`, `cancel`, and `destructive` styles. Use descriptive labels and mark dangerous actions as destructive."},"174":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-7","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert#usage-and-limitations","content":"The shared modal traps focus, isolates the background and restores focus on closing. Check keyboard dismissal and labeling in your composition."},"175":{"id":"/docs/components/alert-8","page_id":"/docs/components/alert","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/alert#usage-and-limitations","content":"The alert stays inside the available viewport and scrolls when text grows or the screen is short. Keep messages concise; use a Sheet for longer tasks."},"176":{"id":"/docs/components/allowshittesting","page_id":"/docs/components/allowshittesting","type":"page","content":"AllowsHitTesting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Components"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/allowshittesting"},"177":{"id":"/docs/components/allowshittesting-0","page_id":"/docs/components/allowshittesting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/allowshittesting","content":"AllowsHitTesting adapts SwiftUI's `allowsHitTesting(_:)` modifier into an explicit interaction wrapper for web overlays and disabled hit regions."},"178":{"id":"/docs/components/allowshittesting-1","page_id":"/docs/components/allowshittesting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/allowshittesting","content":"**Status**: adapted"},"179":{"id":"/docs/components/angulargradient","page_id":"/docs/components/angulargradient","type":"page","content":"AngularGradient","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Components"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/angulargradient"},"180":{"id":"/docs/components/angulargradient-0","page_id":"/docs/components/angulargradient","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/angulargradient","content":"AngularGradient sweeps color stops around a center point."},"181":{"id":"/docs/components/aspectratio","page_id":"/docs/components/aspectratio","type":"page","content":"AspectRatio","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Components"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/aspectratio"},"182":{"id":"/docs/components/aspectratio-0","page_id":"/docs/components/aspectratio","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/aspectratio","content":"Constrains content to a target ratio, aligned with SwiftUI's aspectRatio modifier."},"183":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","type":"page","content":"AsyncImage","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Components"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/asyncimage"},"184":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-0","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/asyncimage","content":"AsyncImage loads remote images and exposes simple loading and failure phases, aligned with SwiftUI's `AsyncImage` mental model."},"185":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-1","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#missing-source","content":"Missing Source"},"186":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-2","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#phase-updates","content":"Phase Updates"},"187":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-3","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#usage-and-limitations","content":"Usage and limitations"},"188":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-4","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#missing-source","content":"When `src` is empty, `AsyncImage` now enters the failure phase immediately instead of attempting a broken browser request:"},"189":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-5","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#phase-updates","content":"Use `onPhaseChange` when the surrounding UI needs to react to loading, success, or failure:"},"190":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-6","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#usage-and-limitations","content":"Use `placeholder` for loading UI."},"191":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-7","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/components/asyncimage#usage-and-limitations","content":"Use `fallback` for failed requests."},"192":{"id":"/docs/components/asyncimage-8","page_id":"/docs/components/asyncimage","tags"